 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to smooth the image and then apply the Sobel operators. Here's how you can modify your code:
```matlab
% Read input image and convert it to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_filtered_image = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation is set to 3 for a 3x3 filter
% Define Sobel operators
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operators to the filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, gaussian filtered image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(gaussian_filtered_image); title('Gaussian Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```
This modified code applies a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function before applying the Sobel edge detection algorithm. The standard deviation of the Gaussian filter is set to 3 for a 3x3 filter, but you can adjust this value based on your specific needs. The resulting edge image will be less affected by noise compared to the original implementation without the Gaussian filter.

